---

25. EMPTY STATES

Every major section needs an intentional empty state.

Examples:

No saved content.

No posts.

No notifications.

No search results.

No following activity.

No watchlist.

Each should have:

- simple visual
- short explanation
- useful action

Do not leave blank black screens.

---

26. ERROR STATES

Errors should be human-readable.

Example:

We couldn't load this.

Retry

Do not expose raw API errors to the user.

Do not display:

"AxiosError: Network Error"

because apparently users enjoy debugging backend infrastructure while trying to find an anime.

---

27. OFFLINE / NETWORK STATES

Where appropriate, show a restrained offline notice.

Previously loaded content should remain usable where the architecture permits.

Network-dependent actions should communicate failure clearly.

---

28. GUEST STATES

Unauthenticated users should not encounter broken screens.

Where authentication is required:

Explain why.

Provide:

Create account

and

Sign in

Do not simply crash, navigate nowhere, or display an empty page.

---

29. MOTION

Motion should communicate:

- orientation
- confirmation
- hierarchy
- connection
- reveal

Do not animate things simply because animation exists.

Important interactions can use:

- subtle scale feedback
- fade
- slide
- shared transitions
- sheet transitions
- spring interactions
- skeleton shimmer
- reaction animation
- spoiler reveal
- tab transitions

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

---

30. INTERACTION QUALITY

This is a critical requirement.

Every interactive element must actually work.

Before considering a screen complete, verify:

- buttons respond
- icons respond
- cards respond
- navigation works
- scrolling works
- sheets open
- dialogs open
- forms accept input
- keyboard behavior works
- back navigation works
- gestures do not block taps
- overlays do not block underlying content
- loading states do not permanently disable controls
- disabled states are intentional
- navigation does not silently fail

Pay particular attention to the existing issue where large portions of the frontend are currently not responding to touch.

Do not solve this by randomly adding "onPress".

Find the actual cause.

Audit:

- Pressable hierarchy
- pointer events
- absolute overlays
- z-index/elevation
- modals
- sheets
- scrims
- PanResponder/gestures
- ScrollView/FlatList interaction
- animated layers
- navigation
- disabled/loading states
- runtime JavaScript errors

A beautiful button that cannot be pressed is just expensive decoration.

