34. DATA FOR LOCAL PREVIEW

This frontend must be previewable even when production services are unavailable.

Use realistic local/mock data where required.

Mock data should resemble actual production data.

For example:

- real-looking entertainment titles
- posters
- actors
- creator profiles
- posts
- comments
- reactions
- communities
- notifications

Do not fill the application with:

“Lorem ipsum”

“Test User”

“Example Show”

unless absolutely necessary.

The local preview should feel like a real Hallyu environment.

However, structure the data layer so replacing local data with the real backend later is straightforward.

---

35. BACKEND BOUNDARY

This task is primarily the frontend implementation.

Do not invent a new backend architecture just to make the UI work.

Use the existing project's architecture and interfaces.

Where production APIs/services are already available, integrate with them appropriately.

Where a backend response is unavailable during local preview, use a clean mock/local data layer.

Keep the boundary between:

UI → state → data/service layer

clean.

---

36. WHAT NOT TO DO

Do NOT:

- generate Figma mockups instead of code
- generate image boards instead of screens
- stop at static UI
- create a fake click-through presentation
- replace Hallyu's brand
- rename the app
- invent a completely different product
- redesign only Home and call it complete
- leave secondary screens as placeholders
- remove existing working features without reason
- introduce unnecessary dependencies
- create a giant component full of unrelated logic
- hardcode every screen separately
- hide errors
- ignore broken interactions
- use tiny unreadable text
- overuse glassmorphism
- overuse gradients
- copy another major entertainment/social app
- treat the old PDF as unquestionable if it conflicts with the current product direction

The old Hallyu specification contains useful interaction and visual principles, but its earlier product scope is narrower than the current Hallyu direction. Use its strongest design principles rather than blindly reproducing its old scope.

